  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current algorithm uses simulated annealing and continuous optimization to find a minimum overlap step function. To improve upon it, we need a new approach that can escape local optima and find more global structures. We'll use a multi-scale approach:\n1. Start with a coarse grid of size n_low\n2. Find good binary patterns with binary simulated annealing (SA)\n3. Upscale those patterns to high resolution\n4. Refine with continuous optimization\n5. Verify with the original parent if available\n\nThe key insight is that overlap minimization is analogous to finding a better way to pack intervals on [0,2] with no self-overlap. Coarse grids may uncover novel patterns that high-resolution searches miss.\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe optimize over piecewise constant functions with possible block sizes S. The objective is:\nC_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nWith constraints:\n0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n\u2211h_i = N/2 (for binary case)\nThe integration is approximated via:\n\u222b = (2/N) * \u2211h_i\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: seed, budget_s, optional initial_h_values\n2. If initial_h_values provided, set best_c5 = compute_c5(initial_h_values), best_h = initial_h_values\n3. Define coarse_grids = [32,64,128] (dynamic scaling if time allows)\n4. For each coarse grid:\n   a. Run 5 independent binary SA searches with random initial states\n   b. Each search: \n        i. Start with random binary assignment (exactly N/2 ones)\n        ii. Perturb by block swaps (size 1-5) with decreasing temperature\n        iii. Track best C_5\n   c. Select best candidate and upsample to 960 using linear interpolation\n   d. Run continuous SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and constraint sum=480\n   e. Compare C_5 across grids and parent\n5. Final return: best_h (upsampled/guessed if parent not available), best_c5, 960\n\nSearch choices:\n- Binary SA: T0=0.1-1.0, T1=0.00001-0.00005, blocks=3, iterations=10^5\n- Continuous: SLSQP with T=10-200, maxiter=400-2000\n- Coresize: start low to high, dynamically adjust if time remains\n\nBudget and robustness:\n- Time budget: 1200 seconds max for grid search + optimization\n- Memory: 960\ud83c\udf2b\ufe0fpoints requires 3d basically (store values and SOBs)\n- Failures: if temperature curves fail, revert to parent; if time low, return best known\n\nValidation:\n1. Compute final C_5 via np.correlate (Numba-accelerated)\n2. Verify \u2211h/2 == N/2 (clip to 1e-6)\n3. If parent available, final candidate must improve\n\n(this checks)\nnp.sum(best_h) \u2248 480? \n\nEvery implementation must:\n1. Use provided helper functions from the parent\n2. Preserve indexing and boundary conditions\n3. Clat the original example from my previous response\n\nLet me detail procedur##al steps:\n1. Perform binary/crystal structure customization (implicit in SA)\n2. Upscale: key interpolation preserving feasibility\n3. Optimization: combine block swaps and continuous polished\n\nComplexity: O(ops * n^2) with 32 grid safe and 960 with optimization taking ~300s\n\nContinue later for complete, but this foundation addresses the problem.",
